ave multiple models you want to compare  them against each other you need to  decide how you want to evaluate them  like do you consider every class as  equally important even though some of  them maybe like maybe some products are  so rare that the model will only see  like one of them a weak and is it really  important the model is extremely  accurate in those  and but then also you might have  requirements from like a  like independent of the bait itself but  coming from business it tells you it's  very important that your model gets this  class right because it  actually means we have to i don't know  do an intervention or  inconvenience a customer or something  like that  so there's  many factors to kind of keep in mind and  now to come back to this second point to  that the distribution that you train the  model on might be different from the one  at test time  so there's a  so  if you assume that after you train a  model  that the probability is that the model  outputs like from the the last layer  which is a softmax in in a normal  classification case  the output is is kind of it looks like a  probability distribution like it sounds  to one you know it varies from zero to  one  um and if you assume that this is  actually a correctly calibrated  probability distribution that tells you  uh  like if the model says 0.8 that means 80  percent of the time it should be correct  so if you assume that's the case  then there's actually a nice way to  convert the model from one distribution  to another  so here on the in the graph on the right  you can see uh first at the training set  and we see in the training sets there's  mostly apples and then  pears and then a few less bananas  so this is the distribution we train on  so the model kind of expects to see more  apples than pears than bananas but then  at test time  we know maybe we  looked at some some logs or we know  something about how what happens in the  store we know that actually the model  will see fewer apples so the  distribution is quite different from the  training distribution and now if you  have a model trained on this training  set and you  apply it on some image and you get an  output like this so the model is very  assured that it's seeing an apple  then this might not actually be the  output that you want because in in the  in in the store at test time you expect  to see less apples maybe the model  should be less confident so what you  want is you want to kind of transform  this output to get a an output that's  kind of adapted to the real distribution  that you know is is their test time and  there's actually a very easy way to do  that and this just follows from bayes  theorem  which is that you can just rescale the  probabilities  uh  probabilities by the ratio of these two  distributions and then you have to  normalize again and then you get this  transformed output and that's actually  assuming that the original model was  well calibrated and this transforms on  with the two  and this is kind of nice because it  means that you can do this kind of under  something for example  to  to have a training set that's easier to  train on and then you can transform the  model to make it adapted to the actual  distribution you would see  a test time  but this kind of assumes that the model  is well calibrated in the first place  which is for for neural networks  i mean there's some indication that it's  sort of true but they're also often  quite over confident so you can't be  sure that the calibration is  uh  is that great  so and and if you assume that it's not  it's not well calibrated well then  instead of just having this ratio  between the two distributions  you can actually think about changing  all these parameters arbitrarily so you  have a  number of classes three parameters that  you can use to add that the model to any  any new distribution  and of course then the question becomes  how do you choose these parameters which  can be quite a few if you h